===Determiner===
{{en-head|det}}

# {{lb|en|interrogative}} {{U|which}}, especially which of an open-ended set of possibilities.
#: {{ux|en|'''What''' colour are you going to use?}}
#: {{ux|en|'''What''' time is it?}}
#: {{ux|en|'''What''' kind of car is that?}}
#: {{ux|en|Did you buy the groceries? — With '''what''' money? We barely have any left.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Justin Curto|title=The 1975’s Matty Healy Prefers Writing Lyrics He’s Afraid Of|magazine={{w|Vulture.com|Vulture}}|url=https://www.vulture.com/2022/10/the-1975-being-funny-in-a-foreign-language-lyrics.html|date=2022-10-14|passage=Everyone always asks '''what''' my advice is, and my advice is don’t overintellectualize your art.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20221018174008/https://www.vulture.com/2022/10/the-1975-being-funny-in-a-foreign-language-lyrics.html}}
# {{lb|en|indirect interrogative}} {{U|which}}.
#: {{ux|en|I wonder '''what''' colour he is going to use.}}
#: {{ux|en|I know '''what''' colour I am going to use.}}
#: {{ux|en|That depends on '''what''' answer is received.}}
# {{lb|en|relative}} {{U|any}} ... that; [[all]] ... that; [[whatever]].
#: {{ux|en|He seems to have lost '''what''' sense he had.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''What''' money I earn is soon spent.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Great Expectations|I|III|40|He crammed '''what''' little food was left, into the breast of his grey jacket.}}
# {{n-g|Emphasises that something is [[noteworthy]] or [[remarkable]] in quality or degree, in either a good or bad way; may be used in combination with certain other determiners, especially 'a', less often 'some'.}}
#: {{ux|en|This shows '''what''' beauty there is in nature.}}
#: {{ux|en|You know '''what''' nonsense she talks.}}
#: {{ux|en|I found out '''what''' a liar he is.}}
## {{lb|en|[[exclamative marker|exclamative]]}} {{n-g|Used to form exclamations indicating that something is remarkable, in either a good or bad way.}}
##: {{syn|en|such}}
##: {{ux|en|'''What''' nonsense!}}
##: {{ux|en|Wow! '''What''' a speech.}}
##: {{ux|en|'''What''' beautiful children you have.}}
##: {{ux|en|With '''what''' passion she sings!}}
##* ''Little Red Riding Hood'', traditional folk tale
##*: “Oh Granny, '''what''' big eyes you have,” said Little Red Riding Hood.

====Usage notes====
In cases where both "what" and "which" are possible, with similar meaning, "what" is preferred for open-ended choices, while "which" is preferred for choices from a closed group or set. For example, "Which one of these do you want?" not "What one of these do you want?".

{{senseid|en|exclamative|tag=p}}As used to begin an exclamation, {{m-self|en|what}} and {{m|en|such}} are largely interchangeable, with a few exceptions:
* Nouns modified by ''such'' need not appear at the beginning of the sentence: ''She sings with such passion.''
* ''such'' requires that the noun phrase it modifies be [[gradable]] in some way. ''Such a disaster!'' is acceptable because a [[disaster]] may be minor or major in degree, but ''Such a movie!'' is not (except with the unusual meaning that the movie under discussion has especially "[[movie]]-like" qualities).
{{m|en|how}} is another word used at the beginning of a sentence to form an exclamation (''How quickly he ran!''), but it modifies different syntactic elements (verbs, adjectives, adverbs, and certain [[determinative]]s).

